WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.632
C programs commonly pass path strings
directly to operating system file APIs.

2
00:00:04.712 --> 00:00:06.277
That directness is powerful,

3
00:00:06.357 --> 00:00:10.676
but it leaves the application responsible
for deciding whether a path is allowed.

4
00:00:10.756 --> 00:00:14.387
The C compiler does not know which
directory is approved for uploads,

5
00:00:14.467 --> 00:00:18.786
which file belongs to a tenant, or which
generated report name should be accepted.

6
00:00:18.866 --> 00:00:22.059
Several pieces of process state can change
what a path means.

7
00:00:22.139 --> 00:00:25.206
The current working directory can affect
relative paths.

8
00:00:25.286 --> 00:00:28.166
Environment variables can influence
configuration.

9
00:00:28.246 --> 00:00:30.687
Process permissions can allow or deny
access.

10
00:00:30.767 --> 00:00:34.899
Platform rules can differ for separators,
case sensitivity, reserved names,

11
00:00:34.979 --> 00:00:37.796
symbolic links, path length, and special
file types.

12
00:00:37.876 --> 00:00:41.131
Secure design treats those facts as part
of the file boundary.

13
00:00:41.211 --> 00:00:44.591
A path may be relative, absolute,
generated by the application,

14
00:00:44.671 --> 00:00:48.991
selected from user input, derived from
archive metadata, copied from a database,

15
00:00:49.071 --> 00:00:50.949
or assembled from multiple fields.

16
00:00:51.029 --> 00:00:53.408
None of those origins is automatically
safe.

17
00:00:53.488 --> 00:00:57.932
What matters is whether the resulting
operation stays inside the approved workflow

18
00:00:58.012 --> 00:00:59.953
and whether the code can explain why.

19
00:01:00.033 --> 00:01:04.164
File system access should therefore be
treated like any other trust boundary.

20
00:01:04.244 --> 00:01:08.313
There should be a clear component that
decides the allowed storage location.

21
00:01:08.393 --> 00:01:11.649
Lower-level helpers should not quietly
reinterpret policy,

22
00:01:11.729 --> 00:01:15.234
and callers should not smuggle policy
through arbitrary strings.

23
00:01:15.314 --> 00:01:19.383
The code should show where untrusted input
stops being raw input and becomes a

24
00:01:19.463 --> 00:01:20.840
validated resource name.

25
00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:22.673
Reviewers need a traceable path.

26
00:01:22.753 --> 00:01:27.010
For each workflow, they should be able to
follow user-influenced data from input

27
00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:30.533
through validation, path joining,
resolution, authorization,

28
00:01:30.613 --> 00:01:32.178
and the final file operation.

29
00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:35.576
That applies to open, read, write, rename,
delete, copy, move,

30
00:01:35.656 --> 00:01:37.471
archive extraction, and cleanup.

31
00:01:37.551 --> 00:01:41.182
If the trace disappears into string
concatenation and assumptions,

32
00:01:41.262 --> 00:01:43.140
the boundary is not visible enough.
